{
  "file": "set-class-and-prime-form.musa",
  "tokens": [
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// A set class is an orbit, and prime form is the name we give it." },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "//" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// Open Music Theory's `102-set-class-and-prime-form.md` states the label two" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// ways. As a recipe: put the set in normal order, transpose it to begin on" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// zero, do the same to its inversion, and keep whichever reads lower. And as a" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// relation: two sets share a label exactly when some transposition or inversion" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// carries one to the other. The recipe is `prime_form`; the relation is an" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// orbit of the T/I group, which `101-pitch-class-sets-normal-order-and-transformations.md`" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// describes one operation at a time. This file computes both and checks that" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// they agree — the triads at twelve carrying one prime form between them, and" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// the orbit at six being exactly as large as the group divided by what fixes" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// the set." },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "//" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// Nothing here is written for twelve. `std::cyclic` names the division and" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// every count follows from it: the T/I group has twice as many operations as" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// the division has pitch classes, and `orbit` and `stabilizer` are" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// `std::algebra`'s, taking that group as the list it is." },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "//" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// **A prime form is compared as a set, not read out as numbers.** `==` at" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// `PcSet` answers the chapter's question — is this the class `(02357)`? — and reading" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// the members back out as numbers builds a `Pc(12)` per member for no gain." },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// The one place the numbers are written out is where the images themselves are" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// the claim." },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "//" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// **The divisions differ between the two halves on purpose.** The recipe runs" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// at twelve, where the chapter's examples are written. The orbit runs at six," },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// because a twenty-four-operation orbit of a twelve-note division costs more" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// reduction steps than `02-core-calculus.md` §4's budget allows today — see" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// prompt 164's Design for the measurement and prompt 165 for whose it is. The" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// theorem is the same at both divisions, which is the whole reason the modulus" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// is a parameter." },
    { "kind": "piece", "text": "piece" },
