    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// Prime form is a property of the set class and not of the set, so" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// inverting the set cannot change it: the losing candidate above was this" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// set read the other way round, which is why it lost rather than naming a" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// second class. The triads below say that in a value, on a set small enough" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// that a second five-member normal order fits the step budget." },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// The triads, which the chapter uses to say what the two relations mean:" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// T₂ of a C major triad is a D major triad, and I₀ of it is an F minor" },
    { "kind": "comment", "text": "// triad. So major and minor triads are one set class, `(037)`." },
